S637 Dehn / Scissors Side-Channel Addendum
================================================

1. Corrected e and pi statement
--------------------------------
Let s=e+pi and p=e*pi.  Since e and pi are transcendental, they cannot both be algebraic: if s and p were algebraic, e and pi would be the two roots of x^2 - s*x + p, hence algebraic over the algebraic numbers, contradiction.

Status table:
claim/status                            reading                             reason
both s and p algebraic                  impossible                          quadratic trap x^2-s*x+p has e,pi as roots
s rational or merely algebraic          would force p transcendental        otherwise both symmetric coordinates are algebraic
p rational or merely algebraic          would force s transcendental        same symmetric-coordinate trap
both s and p transcendental/irrational  open/compatible with known theorem  the theorem proves at least one, not exactly one
exactly one rational                    not known                           possible only as a conditional branch, not a theorem

Reading: addition and multiplication are the elementary symmetric coordinates of the unordered pair {e,pi}.  The theorem is not a parity statement; it is a retained-side-channel statement about algebraic dependence.

2. Rationality is not parity
----------------------------
channel                     closed quotient?                      failure mode / useful replacement
odd/even                    yes: Z -> Z/2                         parity is a genuine two-color ring quotient
rational/irrational         no                                    irrationals are not closed: sqrt(2)+(-sqrt(2)) and sqrt(2)*sqrt(2)
algebraic/transcendental    better, but subtle                    algebraic numbers form a field; transcendence is not closed either
algebraic dependence        best channel here                     (sum,product) can trap a two-element set over a base field
add/multiply                bridged by symmetric polynomials      for positive values logs also convert product to sum, with new side data

3. Cuboids, simplices, and Dehn side channels
----------------------------------------------
regular tetrahedron dihedral angle theta/pi ~= 0.391826552031
Niven check: if theta/pi were rational and cos(theta)=1/3, Niven's rational-angle theorem would force cos(theta) in {0,+/-1/2,+/-1}; contradiction.  Thus theta is nonzero in R/(pi Q).

object                                  volume channel              Dehn / retained channel
cuboid                                  ordinary volume             zero Dehn invariant: right angles vanish in R/(pi Q)
cube triangulated into simplices        same total volume           simplex Dehn pieces cancel in pairs/families
regular tetrahedron                     can match cuboid volume     nonzero Dehn invariant from acos(1/3)
tetrahedra packed in a box              volume inequality only      packing is not scissors congruence unless boundary Dehn data cancels

Reading: volume is the scalar quotient; Dehn data is the side channel.  A simplex can sit inside a cuboid, and a cuboid can be triangulated into simplices, but equal volume alone does not give equidecomposability.

